{{also|Софія}}
==Bulgarian==
{{wikipedia|lang=bg}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|bg|gkm|-}} and {{bor|bg|grc|σοφία||[[wisdom]], [[divine]] [[wisdom]]}}. For the female name, see {{m|en|Sophia}}. As the name of the capital of Bulgaria, from {{m|bg|църква|tr=-}} „{{m|bg|света|Света|tr=-}} София“ (''cǎrkva "Sveta Sofija"''), from {{bor|bg|gkm|ναός|Ναός|tr=-}} {{m|grc|ἁγία|Ἁγίας|tr=-}} {{m|grc|Σοφία|Σοφίας|tr=-}} (''Naós Hagías Sophías'', “[[church|Church]] of [[holy|Holy]] [[wisdom|Wisdom]]”), the city's prominent {{w|Church of St. Sophia|6th-century Byzantine Greek church}}, first attested being used as the name for the city in the 14th century and reinforced under Ottoman rule as {{der|bg|ota|صوفیه|tr=Sofya}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{bg-IPA|Со́фия}}

===Proper noun===
{{bg-proper noun|Со́фия|f|adj=софи́йски}}

# [[Sofia]] ({{given name|bg|female|eq=Sophia|from=Ancient Greek}})
